{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And these are the generations of Aaron and Moses.</b> Moses is mentioned because he taught them Torah, for a person's disciples are his essential descendants: Aaron's descendants were by birth, and Moses' descendants were by the learning of Torah. Therefore Sinai is mentioned, for it was there that Torah was given, and it says <b>on the day that YHWH spoke to Moses on Mount Sinai.</b> And so the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, expounded: whoever teaches his fellow's son Torah, Scripture accounts it to him as if he had begotten him. After that, it mentions ״ואלה שמות בני אהרן״ — 'and these are the names of the sons of Aaron' — to clarify that they were sons of Aaron alone, and therefore it mentions all four of them by name. After that, it mentions a third time: ״אלה שמות בני אהרן הכהנים המשוחים אשר מלא וגו׳״ — 'these are the names of the sons of Aaron, the anointed priests, whom he filled, etc.'" "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret by way of the plain sense (peshat) that this was necessary to teach you that the Holy One, blessed be He, is exacting with the righteous to a hair's breadth, as it is said (Psalms 50:3): ״וסביביו נשערה מאד״ — 'and around Him it is very stormful'; and therefore He added ״הכהנים המשוחים״ — 'the anointed priests' — to say that even great men such as these anointed priests ״אשר מלא ידם לכהן״ — 'whose hands he filled to serve as priests' — He showed no partiality toward them when they sinned before Him, but punished them immediately with the penalty of death. And this is what is written (Deuteronomy 28:50): ״אשר לא ישא פנים לזקן״ — 'who will not show partiality to an elder,' and it is written (Job 34:19): ״אשר לא נשא פני שרים ולא נכר שוע לפני דל כי מעשה ידיו כלם״ — 'who shows no partiality to princes, and does not regard the rich more than the poor, for they are all the work of His hands.'" "<b>And it is possible</b> further that there is a secret in the verse, for בני אהרן — 'the sons of Aaron' — refers back to Nadab and Abihu alone, who had already died. This is why it mentions ״וימת נדב ואביהו לפני ה׳״ — 'and Nadab and Abihu died before YHWH' — and then says ״הכהנים המשוחים״ — 'the anointed priests'; and since all four of them had been anointed, it was necessary to specify ״אשר מלא ידם לכהן״ — read it as לכהן — 'to serve as a priest,' that is to say, those whose hands he filled to serve as another priest, and this is Phinehas son of Eleazar. And this is what is written (Exodus 6:25): ״ותלד לו את פינחס אלה וגו׳״ — 'and she bore him Phinehas; these are, etc.' And this is why the Torah mentions the death of Nadab and Abihu in the section of Phinehas, and this is what is written at the beginning of the section of Phinehas: ״פינחס בן אלעזר בן אהרן״ — 'Phinehas son of Eleazar son of Aaron.' For the entire Torah is allusions and hints. Therefore it mentions here three times אלה, to allude to three modes of coming-into-being (הויות): the first, an intellectual coming-into-being with respect to Moses; the second, a physical one with respect to Aaron; and the third, a renewed coming-into-being according to the attributes of the Holy One, blessed be He. And because two of these comings-into-being were at one time, therefore He mentioned them with ואלה — with a joining vav — whereas in the renewed coming-into-being He mentioned אלה without a vav. And since He mentioned them, He explained their death and their sin, that they died on account of it."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:18.087443650Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}